                "text": "Testing the gravity rule against example 1: counting 8s above the line gives 10, but none appear above in the output, while below the line the count grows from 30 to 40 \u2014 suggesting those 10 8s fell through the gap in the barrier line down to the lower region.\n\nChecking example 2 confirms the same pattern: 10 8s above plus 24 below sums to 34, matching the output total, and the fallen 8s stack in a row centered on the gap position rather than aligned to one side.\n\nExample 3 adds complexity with both a horizontal and vertical divider, splitting the grid into three regions; tracing the counts shows the 8s from above fall through the gap into the left region and pile up at the bottom, with the second-from-bottom row filled in a centered fashion \u2014 confirming the centering behavior seen in example 2. I'm now moving on to check example 4 for further confirmation of this centered-fill, gravity-through-gap rule.\n\nChecking total 8-count conservation instead: input has 5+46=51, output would need 2+1+48=51, which matches. So the lower region fills to full capacity and the overflow of 3 spills upward through the gap column, but the placement isn't centered\u2014I need to check other examples to see how the overflow cells are arranged relative to the gap position.\n\nMaybe the spread is centered within the region rather than anchored to the gap: ex3's remainder of 2 centers as cols 2,3 in a width-6 region, and ex2's remainder of 4 centers as cols 3-6 in width-10, which fits. But ex4 breaks this pattern since its pile only spans partial rows, so maybe gravity direction isn't always downward \u2014 perhaps it's toward whichever line orientation applies, though ex1 shows both regions falling down normally.\n\nI'm testing whether the pile centers under the gap, but the observed positions in ex3 and ex4 don't match a centered placement, so that hypothesis seems wrong. Trying instead whether the pile forms adjacent to the gap column rather than